        ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}-8xy=3{x}^{2}y$ for $y\\ne 0.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}-8xy & =3{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8xy & =3{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}y+8xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+8x)y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}+8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y| & ={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y|={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y| & ={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}.\n$$",
